{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Looking at Code Coverage for the First Time"}, {"type": "position", "content": 9}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about code coverage."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe ways of solving problems and getting things done in written and spoken situations and in work and social contexts. Train the system users on application structuring and management, according to the established plan and user profile, according to the organization policies."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace.  Recognizes simple ideas on managing a proposed application, using available hardware for an application, and technical reports on an IT solution."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob09re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "What Clauses and Long Noun Phrases as Subjects"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We can use <span class=\"strong\">what clauses</span> and long <span class=\"strong\">noun phrases</span> as subjects when we want to give more information about the subject of a sentence. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Let's start by remembering that a <span class=\"strong\">clause</span> always contains a subject and a verb. This clause can either work as an independent clause (it has a meaning by itself and doesn't need any extra information) or a dependent clause (does not have a meaning by itself and depends on another clause)."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">What</span> clauses are dependent clauses and can work as the subject of a sentence. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example: <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">What defect metrics does</span> is to inform how many defects were found in a product.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "In this example, the part of the sentence that is in bold is the subject of the main verb of the sentence, the verb is. Notice that in the <span class=\"strong\">what</span> clause, we have a subject <span class=\"strong\">defect metrics</span> and a verb <span class=\"strong\">does</span>. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Usually, the verb used for the subject clause is going to be the verb <span class=\"italic\">to be</span>. Nevertheless, other verbs are also possible. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example: <span class=\"italic\">What we wanted to modify about the code <span class=\"strong\">interested</span> the managers.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "In this last example, the main verb of the sentence, is the verb <span class=\"italic\">to interest</span>. Note that we can conjugate the main verb of the sentence in different tenses, but as the subject of the verb is going to be a clause, the verb must be conjugated in the third person singular.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "A long noun phrase as a subject, refers to a group of words that form a noun phrase that has the function of subject in a sentence."}, {"type": "text", "content": " Example: <span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">Knowing how to track defects</span> is very important for every organization.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "In this example, <span class=\"italic\">knowing how to track defects</span> is the subject of the verb <span class=\"strong\">is</span>. For a noun phrase, just as in a <span class=\"strong\">what</span> clause, we are going to conjugate the verb of the noun phrase in the third person singular. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "What code coverage metrics does is give feedback on how much of the code was tested. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Knowing what parts of the code were left untested helps us determine if it's necessary to test the remaining code."}, {"type": "example", "content": "What the results presented was that I had only covered 60% of the code instead of the 90% I was expecting. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Understanding the Concept of Unit Testing"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Unit testing is a method for testing code in which each individual unit of code is tested separately to detect errors. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Unit testing consists of taking the smallest testable piece of code and testing it to determine if it works as expected or not. When doing so, the testers can quickly identify defects and fix them easily. If a tester tests a more complex piece of code, finding the source of that defect becomes harder. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Unit testing can be more time consuming and more expensive. However, it reduces the difficulties in spotting defects in more complex pieces of code and test coverage is improved as more attention is given to each unit. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "For unit testing, testers will often have to develop stubs and drivers. As testers will only test one unit at a time in this method, they still have to take into consideration how this unit is going to function in a certain context. For this reason, they will develop stubs, which are simulated units for sub programs; and drivers, that simulate unites for main programs. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "leave off "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to stop on a certain point"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We left off at the code coverage chapter. Let's review some important concepts."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob09aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "error checking "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a piece of code that is used for finding errors"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: What I concluded was that I didn't test the added error checking code; and nevertheless, the application worked fine."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob09aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "examine"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to inspect, to test"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: What a tester will do in a unit testing process is examine if the code behaves as expected by writing drivers and stubs, and test each individual unit code."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob09aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "lecture"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a talk given to a class related to a subject in a determined amount of time"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Last lecture, we left off at the code coverage chapter."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob09aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "hence"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "therefore, for this reason"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(connector)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Hence, after knowing what part of the code was left untested, we need to think whether it's useful to test the rest of it or not."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob09aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "compulsory"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "necessary, obligatory"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It's not compulsory to test all the code."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob09aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Read about unit testing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Describe how it works."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write a small essay about its functions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Show your essay to your classmates."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I read about unit testing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I describe how it works."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write an essay."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I show my essay."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can say what code coverage metrics provide feedback on. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can refer to the concept of unit testing in regards to coverage metrics. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can discuss an experience related to code coverage."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you think it is important to test every piece of code when developing software? Why? Why not?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you think it's better to test a complex piece of code or basic units of code? Why?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "What Code Coverage Really Measures"}], "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "img_path": ""}, {"stimuli_ids": [1], "temp